WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.442 --> 00:00:08.630
It was about 12 years ago

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:10.603
that I first heard of this machine.

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:14.140
It's a brain slicer,

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.180
but not just any old brain slicer

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:18.240
that you'd find at Home Depot

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:22.270
or your local serial killer hobby shop.

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:26.360
This is the best brain
slicer in the world.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:28.823
And it lives at Harvard University.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:32.303
This machine may sound medieval,

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:36.457
but it's at the heart of
one of the grand scientific

00:00:36.457 --> 00:00:39.133
and computing challenges of our time.

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:41.990
Researchers here want to use it

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:44.910
to help them build a three-dimensional map

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:46.113
of the human brain.

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:50.600
They want to photograph
a brain slice by slice,

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:54.123
stitch all the pictures
together into a computer model,

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:58.480
and then go find every
neuron and every synapse

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.313
and the trillions of
pathways that connect them.

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:05.753
Scientists call this
brain map the connectome,

00:01:06.883 --> 00:01:09.780
and some of them are convinced
it could unlock the secrets

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.193
of how the mind works.

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:14.450
Other people think this whole project

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:17.183
is a colossal waste of money and time.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:23.110
Luckily for scientists and for
you, I have come to Harvard

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:26.290
to settle this matter once and for all.

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:27.783
To the connectome.

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:42.390
Whenever I'm at Harvard,

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:45.540
thinking about the origins
of human consciousness

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:49.373
and the mechanics of
life, I visit this man.

00:01:51.614 --> 00:01:52.447
I'm Jeff Lichtman.

00:01:52.447 --> 00:01:54.110
I'm a professor in the molecular

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:57.070
and cellular biology department
at Harvard University

00:01:57.070 --> 00:02:01.023
and a member of a group called
The Center for Brain Science.

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:02.723
Jeff is also

00:02:02.723 --> 00:02:05.270
one of the world's
leading connectome experts

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:06.960
and runs a laboratory here.

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:11.290
This idea that there is a connectome,

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:13.940
a wiring diagram that is worth knowing,

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:18.370
it remains controversial to
this day. To think about a map,

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:20.980
a physical map of the brain's connections

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:25.100
very much the way people in
genetics thought of the genome

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:29.160
as a physical map of all
the sequence information

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:32.220
for every gene in a body.

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:34.850
Genomics at its early stages,

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:37.100
there was also some
controversy over whether

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:38.680
it was really a good idea.

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:41.240
It was also considered a huge data set.

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:45.970
It was about 1.4 gigabytes.
Amazing.

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:47.467
1.4 gigabytes, and people were thinking,

00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:50.270
"What are we going to
do with so much data?"

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:54.210
But the idea here is that
the technologies now exist

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:57.100
to get wiring diagrams down to the level

00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:00.790
of every single connection
of every single nerve cell

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:02.320
to every other nerve cell.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:05.365
You're basically talking
about finding every neuron,

00:03:05.365 --> 00:03:08.610
every synapse, all these
connections between them.

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:10.480
And so we're talking about on the order

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:13.580
of trillions and trillions of wires.

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:14.910
Oh yeah.
Yeah.

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:16.870
It's almost inconceivable.

00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:18.910
To get a wiring diagram of the brain,

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:22.490
you'd need enough resolution
to see every single synapse.

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:25.150
And that resolution is lower

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:27.090
than the resolution of light microscopes.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:28.340
It's controversial.

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:30.790
What's the great pro case
for why we want to do this?

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:33.790
You see the United States
has put tons of money

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:35.100
in trying to do a connectome.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:36.380
Europe has put tons of money,

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:37.930
so obviously there's a group of people

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:39.940
that believe this is a big deal.

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:41.940
What is the big hope?

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:45.200
Maybe the one that is
most generally interesting

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:48.280
is that there are a number
of disorders of brains

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:51.550
where we still have a very poor idea

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:53.160
of what actually is wrong.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:55.000
And a number of people believe

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:59.080
that the reason these
diseases are so difficult

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:01.580
is that they go to the
structure of the brain.

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:04.860
And we have really no idea
what those pathologies are.

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:08.800
Diseases that are not uncommon,
autism spectrum disorder,

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:11.720
schizophrenia, Tourette's syndrome.

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:13.210
I don't think we're going to get very far

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:16.570
until we have some physical
evidence of what's wrong.

00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:18.640
Memory in its true sense,

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:20.870
in the sense of a
neuroscientist thinking about it

00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:23.960
or the way a normal person
thinks about what they remember,

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:25.720
where is that? What is that?

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:28.840
What does it look like? How
is it configured in the brain?

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:31.690
It's almost certainly
in the wiring diagram,

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:33.990
but until we start
getting wiring diagrams,

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:35.790
we're not going to have a
good idea what they are.

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:40.010
A brain schematic

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:42.203
sounds like an easy enough thing to build,

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.520
but in the decade plus
that I've been following

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:48.320
connectome projects around the globe,

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:50.713
progress has proven tough to come by.

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:55.810
Researchers have managed to
build models of tiny worm brains

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:58.640
and slightly larger fly brains.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:01.900
The goal these days is to
image an entire mouse brain.

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:05.263
And then one day far in
the future, a human brain.

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:09.410
It turns out that our
brains are so complex

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:12.640
that they require an
almost unprecedented amount

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:14.463
of computing to analyze.

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:18.960
You guys recently did a
cerebral cortex in a human.

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:19.850
But a cubic millimeter.

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:20.683
A cubic millimeter of that.

00:05:20.683 --> 00:05:22.300
But that's how hard this is, right?

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:23.800
How long has it, how long have you--

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:25.860
That took several years.
Several years.

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:27.910
And two teams, a big team at Google

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:31.680
and a big team in my lab
working full-time on this.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:35.580
The problem here is that
the wires are not ordered.

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:37.380
They're not all running like train tracks

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:38.213
in the same direction.

00:05:38.213 --> 00:05:40.140
They're running in three-dimensional space

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:43.580
more the way spaghetti
in a bowl would run.

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:48.580
One way of generating a map of
every wire in every direction

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:52.080
is to cut that bowl of
spaghetti, piece of brain,

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:55.180
into very thin slices, take
each of those pictures,

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:58.210
and then align them, and
then find piece of spaghetti,

00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:00.640
and track it through the volume,

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:02.350
and then start with
another piece of spaghetti

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:03.430
and track it through the volume

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:06.880
and keep doing that until
you have all the spaghetti.

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:09.500
These are 30 nanometer sections typically,

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:13.470
which is about a thousandth
the thickness of a human hair.

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:14.693
Each section.

00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:21.390
The first step in building a connectome

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:25.413
is to get your hands on some
brains and then cut them up.

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:28.250
It all sounds very grim,

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:30.490
but Harvard turns out
to be quite efficient

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:32.760
with its brain harvesting.

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:35.320
Jeff's lab can do a
year's worth of research

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:36.733
with a single mouse brain.

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:40.650
Small chunks of the brain
are fed into the slicer

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:44.810
where a diamond blade carves
off a thin sliver of tissue.

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:47.040
The tissue floats along some liquid

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:48.583
and onto a piece of tape.

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:54.090
The brain slices are then put
into this very large machine

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:57.860
where they're photographed
in the finest of detail.

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:00.730
Software helps stitch the
pictures back together

00:07:00.730 --> 00:07:02.860
into a single 3D image

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:05.600
and algorithms are
applied to help make sense

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:07.883
of what's going on inside of the brain.

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:11.870
This summer, Lichtman's
lab along with Google

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:13.750
announced their major breakthrough

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:16.440
on the human connectome front.

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:20.120
They created a 1.4 petabyte visual model

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:23.960
from one cubic millimeter of human cortex.

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:27.200
Within that tiny sample
were millions of neurons

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:30.033
and 130 million synapses.

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:33.930
All of these parts were
then labeled by an obedient

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:36.183
and hardworking AI algorithm.

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:40.450
Whose brain sample is it?

00:07:40.450 --> 00:07:41.810
Was there something--

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:46.200
Oh, it's a woman who had had epilepsy.

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:47.390
And she had surgery or something.

00:07:47.390 --> 00:07:51.080
She had surgery, and to
remove the epileptic focus,

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:54.560
the neurosurgeons made an
incision in the temporal lobe

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:57.390
to get access to the disease site

00:07:57.390 --> 00:08:01.820
so they could extirpate
or in some way remove

00:08:01.820 --> 00:08:04.593
the part of the brain that
was causing the epilepsy.

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:06.803
If we zoom up,

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:10.540
every one of these objects

00:08:11.650 --> 00:08:16.650
is now a uniquely identified
object in the data.

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:19.170
There's hundreds of
millions of objects in here.

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:21.510
If I take an object like this

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:23.320
that has a sort of triangular shape

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:25.050
that looks like a pyramidal neuron,

00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:26.853
and I just double click on it.

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:28.713
There it is.

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:31.490
It almost looks like a cartoon,
but that's the actual--

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:32.810
This is a real cell.

00:08:32.810 --> 00:08:37.450
And if I do that for
this cell, there is it.

00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:40.873
And if I do it for this cell,

00:08:42.230 --> 00:08:43.860
I'm going to have to unzoom a little bit

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:45.660
to give you a sense of this.

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:47.310
You're making the bowl of spaghetti.

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:50.500
And everything in there is traceable.

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:53.510
That's a place where a
cell receives a synapse.

00:08:53.510 --> 00:08:55.310
And then I'm going to zoom up on that spot

00:08:55.310 --> 00:08:57.420
in the electron micrograph.

00:08:57.420 --> 00:09:02.420
Here's an axon making a synapse
on that dendritic spine.

00:09:03.270 --> 00:09:05.320
And I can do that for every one of these.

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:08.013
Let's look at this.

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:13.220
This is something I saw with my own eyes

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:15.100
and then asked my colleagues

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:17.350
if they'd ever seen anything like this,

00:09:17.350 --> 00:09:18.183
and no one had.

00:09:18.183 --> 00:09:20.100
So here is a axon.

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:23.970
That's something that's
talking, where the dendrites

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:26.300
of the cell are in red.

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:28.400
That's the cell that's listening.

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:31.440
And this axon comes to
this position on the cell

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:33.550
and makes something that
looks sort of like a turbine.

00:09:33.550 --> 00:09:38.370
It's a massive wiring weirdness.

00:09:38.370 --> 00:09:41.903
If you follow this axon,
it keeps doing this.

00:09:43.330 --> 00:09:47.980
Here's the same axon, another whirl.

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:49.940
And that's the thing about large data.

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:52.360
These things that are called unicorns,

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:55.240
these weird things that there's one of,

00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:57.750
when you have 1.4 petabytes of data,

00:09:57.750 --> 00:09:59.650
you can discover that they're rare,

00:09:59.650 --> 00:10:01.850
but they're not one-offs.

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:05.580
Do we have any speculation
about why this happens?

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:08.350
One person in my lab, Richard Schaleck,

00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:13.245
suggested that maybe
this is a magnetic coil,

00:10:13.245 --> 00:10:15.120
that the electricity travels here

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:17.660
and it generates a magnetic charge.

00:10:17.660 --> 00:10:22.410
I think that's a crazy
idea, but he took offense.

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:24.015
He said, "You got a better idea?"

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:29.000
Not everyone at Harvard

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:31.303
is a full-on connectome believer.

00:10:33.370 --> 00:10:35.830
So, for another view on the matter,

00:10:35.830 --> 00:10:40.690
may I please present to you
the bad boy of brain science,

00:10:40.690 --> 00:10:42.630
Florian Engert.

00:10:42.630 --> 00:10:44.693
This man knows how to make an entrance,

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:49.153
Ashlee!
Hello.

00:10:50.725 --> 00:10:51.625
Good to see you.

00:10:52.550 --> 00:10:54.360
How are you?
I'm well.

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:55.619
Good.

00:10:55.619 --> 00:10:57.203
It's a lovely day.
It is a nice day.

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:00.520
Is that how you usually cruise around?

00:11:00.520 --> 00:11:02.860
That's how I commute.

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:06.000
Sitting here slotted between two rhinos,

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:08.270
I got right into it and asked this man

00:11:08.270 --> 00:11:12.770
in a purple muscle tee what
he makes of brain maps.

00:11:12.770 --> 00:11:15.285
There's controversies
around the connectome

00:11:15.285 --> 00:11:18.190
to some degree of some people,

00:11:18.190 --> 00:11:19.620
there's people who think it will be useful

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:21.290
in different ways, so
there's some debates there.

00:11:21.290 --> 00:11:22.880
Then there's a camp of people who think

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:24.960
this is maybe not a waste of time,

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:27.810
but it's not going to lead,
yeah, so where do you fall?

00:11:27.810 --> 00:11:30.650
Is the connectome a worthwhile pursuit?

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:32.570
Well, it's not going to be sufficient.

00:11:32.570 --> 00:11:34.850
Maybe that's the most important statement.

00:11:34.850 --> 00:11:37.540
Just having the connectome by itself

00:11:37.540 --> 00:11:39.610
will tell you basically nothing.

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:43.213
I think it is extremely useful,

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:48.460
once you have a model, a theory
of how it works, the brain,

00:11:48.460 --> 00:11:51.450
and you want to use the
connectome to falsify

00:11:51.450 --> 00:11:53.253
or test or constrain your model.

00:11:59.570 --> 00:12:01.300
Florian's lab specializes

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:04.063
in researching the brains of zebra fish.

00:12:04.930 --> 00:12:06.810
And he has a glorious collection

00:12:06.810 --> 00:12:08.593
of tens of thousands of them.

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:13.290
These fish turn out to be
ideal for brain research.

00:12:13.290 --> 00:12:16.143
Just after they're born,
they have translucent skin.

00:12:17.270 --> 00:12:19.610
This means that scientists
can peer directly

00:12:19.610 --> 00:12:23.013
into the animals' minds and
watch their neurons fire.

00:12:24.340 --> 00:12:27.350
Florian and his team of
over-educated fish farmers

00:12:27.350 --> 00:12:29.960
have altered the genes of these animals

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:31.550
so that their neuronal activity

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:33.973
really lights up under a microscope.

00:12:35.700 --> 00:12:38.257
We have maybe a few thousand

00:12:38.257 --> 00:12:40.830
different transgenic fish lines,

00:12:40.830 --> 00:12:43.550
the different populations
of fish that we need

00:12:43.550 --> 00:12:46.380
to monitor and manipulate neural activity

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:47.910
in individual parts of the brain.

00:12:47.910 --> 00:12:48.950
And that's what you see here.

00:12:48.950 --> 00:12:53.030
And so each case would maybe
have a different property?

00:12:53.030 --> 00:12:54.210
You need more than one row.

00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:56.380
So from here to here,

00:12:56.380 --> 00:13:00.510
they would all express
a fluorescent indicator

00:13:00.510 --> 00:13:02.540
in a very specific part of the brain.

00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:06.090
And other fish would express
in different areas of the brain

00:13:06.090 --> 00:13:07.550
or a different indicator.

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:10.040
Who keeps track of what happens?

00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:11.650
You just have them all
numbered and somebody's--

00:13:11.650 --> 00:13:14.230
Oh no, we have a bar coding system.

00:13:14.230 --> 00:13:17.700
There's a whole database
that needs to get updated.

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:22.700
And we have a staff of three
people and a head fish mistress

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:24.610
who takes care of the whole facility.

00:13:24.610 --> 00:13:27.950
And she keeps track of
maintenance and housekeeping.

00:13:27.950 --> 00:13:29.950
So when you're looking
at this brain activity,

00:13:29.950 --> 00:13:32.160
it's the first couple days of their life.

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:35.400
It's day five till day eight.

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:40.400
Day five or day six with zebra
fish has a whole repertoire

00:13:40.630 --> 00:13:43.410
of behaviors that they need to survive.

00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:46.040
That's what they do, they
interact with conspecifics.

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:49.083
They hunt, they escape
competitors and they navigate.

00:13:50.920 --> 00:13:52.240
Florian was too important

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:54.700
to explain how the
actual microscoping part

00:13:54.700 --> 00:13:56.420
of all this works.

00:13:56.420 --> 00:14:00.500
And so poor post-doc
Gregor stepped in to help.

00:14:00.500 --> 00:14:01.370
I can get a fish in a dish

00:14:01.370 --> 00:14:03.570
and then carry it into the microscope

00:14:03.570 --> 00:14:05.650
and then kind of set up an experiment.

00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:08.910
First Gregor embeds the fish

00:14:08.910 --> 00:14:11.570
in the laboratory equivalent of jello.

00:14:11.570 --> 00:14:13.550
Then he strips away bits of the jello

00:14:13.550 --> 00:14:17.550
to let the zebra fish
move its body and tail.

00:14:17.550 --> 00:14:19.800
The idea is to see how the brain works

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:24.180
in a living animal going about
its day-to-day activities.

00:14:24.180 --> 00:14:27.480
And so you can see that
when I tap the dish,

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:31.970
and it'll do this little tail flick.

00:14:31.970 --> 00:14:35.186
To see the neurons, you would
use a different microscope,

00:14:35.186 --> 00:14:37.050
more high powered machine?

00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:37.883
Yep.
Okay.

00:14:37.883 --> 00:14:40.970
So you do this and then you
take it and then image deeper.

00:14:40.970 --> 00:14:41.920
Exactly.
Okay.

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:46.760
The scientists often use light
patterns to trick the fish

00:14:46.760 --> 00:14:49.380
into thinking that they're
swimming in a stream

00:14:49.380 --> 00:14:51.390
or running into an obstacle.

00:14:51.390 --> 00:14:53.710
It's like fish VR.

00:14:53.710 --> 00:14:55.890
Because the dots are
changing which way they go,

00:14:55.890 --> 00:14:57.930
so the fish is going to be
kind of changing its pattern.

00:14:57.930 --> 00:14:58.763
Yeah, exactly.

00:14:58.763 --> 00:15:00.790
Fish are gonna swim left, right.

00:15:00.790 --> 00:15:02.490
This is brain activity that you see.

00:15:02.490 --> 00:15:05.140
All these purple and orange dots,

00:15:05.140 --> 00:15:07.290
these are individual cell bodies.

00:15:07.290 --> 00:15:08.123
And whenever they flash,

00:15:08.123 --> 00:15:09.920
that means these cells become active.

00:15:10.950 --> 00:15:13.620
In a way, Florian's fish
study is the opposite

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:15.730
of the connectome approach.

00:15:15.730 --> 00:15:19.220
Rather than trying to untangle
the whole bowl of spaghetti,

00:15:19.220 --> 00:15:22.940
his lab can zero in on the
specific spots in the brain

00:15:22.940 --> 00:15:25.500
that light up in response to stimuli,

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:27.660
and then start to develop a theory

00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:29.593
of what fishy thoughts look like.

00:15:30.740 --> 00:15:32.383
Can you give me an
example along those lines?

00:15:32.383 --> 00:15:34.040
A zebra fish does X,

00:15:34.040 --> 00:15:36.040
and I could see that in the brain

00:15:36.040 --> 00:15:37.430
and find out whether
that's right or wrong.

00:15:37.430 --> 00:15:40.620
For example, one of the
things we are studying

00:15:40.620 --> 00:15:41.740
is working memory.

00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:44.260
So the fish sees a pulse of motion.

00:15:44.260 --> 00:15:47.340
And it remembers that for a few seconds.

00:15:47.340 --> 00:15:49.400
And now we've identified a brain region

00:15:49.400 --> 00:15:52.900
where this information, this
activity is being stored

00:15:52.900 --> 00:15:55.310
in a recurrent circuit,
so we can see by imaging,

00:15:55.310 --> 00:15:57.070
by recording activity

00:15:57.070 --> 00:15:59.710
that there's information in
this ventral hindbrain circuit

00:15:59.710 --> 00:16:02.430
of the fish where this information stays.

00:16:02.430 --> 00:16:05.070
So we have sort of have
found now a nucleus

00:16:05.070 --> 00:16:07.680
for working memory where this information

00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:09.550
is being stored transiently.

00:16:09.550 --> 00:16:14.030
I think the critical part
is you need to know already

00:16:14.030 --> 00:16:16.070
how it works, and then
you can use it to test.

00:16:16.070 --> 00:16:17.350
Many of my friends and colleagues,

00:16:17.350 --> 00:16:19.680
they won't like what I say now.

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:21.800
So if you don't know how it works,

00:16:21.800 --> 00:16:24.420
then having the connectome
is going to hurt you

00:16:24.420 --> 00:16:25.410
more than it's going to help.

00:16:25.410 --> 00:16:29.070
It's going to be much more
confusing than illuminating.

00:16:29.070 --> 00:16:30.300
Should we be spending money

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:35.300
to even try to do a human
connectome just because we can?

00:16:36.138 --> 00:16:36.971
Well, no.

00:16:36.971 --> 00:16:38.220
I think we should absolutely.

00:16:38.220 --> 00:16:39.970
It's about expectation management.

00:16:39.970 --> 00:16:42.230
We should not do the human connectome,

00:16:42.230 --> 00:16:43.960
the same we shouldn't have done the genome

00:16:43.960 --> 00:16:48.010
for the purpose of using
it to directly cure disease

00:16:48.010 --> 00:16:49.385
or help the human condition

00:16:49.385 --> 00:16:50.935
because that's not going to work.

00:16:50.935 --> 00:16:52.930
But what it's useful for

00:16:52.930 --> 00:16:56.740
is to help the existing
science infrastructure

00:16:56.740 --> 00:16:59.410
to constrain the models.

00:16:59.410 --> 00:17:03.450
It's another additional
tool that will help you

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:04.930
with incremental insight.

00:17:04.930 --> 00:17:06.730
Most of the wealthiest people
in the world these days

00:17:06.730 --> 00:17:08.970
are tech people and they're
interested in stuff.

00:17:08.970 --> 00:17:10.337
It seemed like, well, we can do this

00:17:10.337 --> 00:17:11.590
and we can do it a lot faster

00:17:11.590 --> 00:17:14.190
if we just had 50 high-powered microscopes

00:17:14.190 --> 00:17:15.170
and more brains slices.

00:17:15.170 --> 00:17:17.840
I'm surprised no really wealthy person--

00:17:17.840 --> 00:17:20.410
It's interesting where the brain stacks up

00:17:20.410 --> 00:17:24.020
versus the body on some of
the priorities of research.

00:17:24.020 --> 00:17:25.900
It's not clear what you would do with it.

00:17:25.900 --> 00:17:27.540
I think maybe that's the problem.

00:17:27.540 --> 00:17:29.120
If you had a billionaire

00:17:29.120 --> 00:17:31.117
and say, "I can give you a connectome."

00:17:32.450 --> 00:17:34.147
Her question would be,

00:17:34.147 --> 00:17:35.450
"What am I going to do with that?"

00:17:35.450 --> 00:17:38.170
What you will you do once
you have the connectome?

00:17:38.170 --> 00:17:41.940
And I think one of the most
prevalent applications,

00:17:41.940 --> 00:17:46.410
which I think is batshit
crazy is to upload.

00:17:46.410 --> 00:17:48.167
Oh, to upload your mind?

00:17:48.167 --> 00:17:50.444
Whatever connectome that that is,

00:17:50.444 --> 00:17:51.277
the substrate of your mind,

00:17:51.277 --> 00:17:54.860
that you can then actually
have uploaded into a computer

00:17:54.860 --> 00:17:56.260
and emulate your mind.

00:17:56.260 --> 00:17:59.560
There's a lot of rich
people who are after that,

00:17:59.560 --> 00:18:01.300
I think an uploading of my brain

00:18:01.300 --> 00:18:04.171
it's more like making
a backup copy, right,

00:18:04.171 --> 00:18:07.092
that is disjointed from my own identity.

00:18:07.092 --> 00:18:09.033
There's really a philosophical flaw,

00:18:09.033 --> 00:18:11.274
a wrong way of thinking,

00:18:11.274 --> 00:18:14.570
that a copy is the same as a continuation

00:18:14.570 --> 00:18:16.430
of my own life.

00:18:16.430 --> 00:18:19.560
So that's why I think all of
this is complete nonsense.

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:23.400
This idea of the connectome
is a tool to upload my mind

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:27.033
into a computer and achieve
immortality is crazy.

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:32.700
After spending a couple of days

00:18:32.700 --> 00:18:36.540
talking about brains, I took
a slow walk around Harvard

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:39.930
to feel what Ivy League life is like.

00:18:39.930 --> 00:18:42.243
As I sauntered majestically,

00:18:42.243 --> 00:18:46.390
I thought about Mark Zuckerberg
building a dating site here,

00:18:46.390 --> 00:18:49.263
and then ending up as our digital emperor.

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:53.090
I thought about how weird it
is that we're totally dependent

00:18:53.090 --> 00:18:56.120
on Google's huge computers and algorithms

00:18:56.120 --> 00:18:58.423
to do so much cutting edge research.

00:19:00.360 --> 00:19:03.000
And I thought about my director, David,

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:05.963
running with a rollerblading
brain scientist.

00:19:06.950 --> 00:19:09.880
I thought about a lot of things, and well,

00:19:09.880 --> 00:19:13.223
I reckon it would be nice
if we knew how that happens.

